Compile/run:
     - Compile using
            make
     - run executable with
            ./CalcYouLater

Purpose
    The CalcYouLater program is meant to read in commands from various sources
    which are generally in RPN notation to perform various math and logic 
    operations that can be output to the user through the help of the 
    implementation of a stack of Datum variables that can hold booleans, 
    rstrings, or integers.

Acknowledgements
    Special thanks to the TAs who provided clarifications on file I/O 
    operations during lab sessions.

Files:

DatumStack.h
    Defines the DatumStack class and its member functions which manage and 
    organize Datum objects into a stack data structure.

DatumStack.cpp
    Implements the DatumStack class, which manages datum objects using a stack
    structure.

RPNCalc.h:
    Acts as the interface for the RPNCalc program and the users inputs 
    meant for use by an outside user with little to no programming knowledge. 

RPNCalc.cpp:
    PURPOSE: Implementation of the RPNCalc class, which holds a stack of datum
    objects. This class provides functionality to perform various arithmetic, 
    boolean, and organizaitonal commands on the data within the stack.

README:
    Acts as a way to navigate and understand the program as a whole including 
    details on architectural and data structure design.

unit_test.h:
    Contains unit tests for various program functionalities, ensuring 
    correctness.

Makefile:
    Automates the compilation process for the program.

test_input.cylc, test_input2.cylc, simple_tests.cylc, complex_tests.cylc,
fact.cylc, fib.cylc, n_fibs.cylc, fib_debug.cylc:
    Various different input files used for testing against template model. 

Architectural Overview:
    The RPNCalc class implements a Reverse Polish Notation (RPN) calculator
    using a stack-based architecture to process and evaluate expressions.
    At the core of the design is the DatumStack, a stack that holds Datum
    objects representing integers, booleans, or RPN strings. The RPNCalc class
    maintains a control flag (cont) for execution flow and processes user
    inputs through run(), which continuously reads and pushes parsed tokens
    to the stack. Commands such as arithmetic (doMath()), logical operations
    (relate(), equate()), and stack manipulations (swap(), drop(), dup())
    interact directly with DatumStack. Complex expressions, encapsulated in
    RPN strings, are recursively parsed (parse()) and executed (exec()),
    allowing for nested evaluations. The program also supports conditional
    execution (stackIf()) and file reading (file()), where inputs from files
    are treated as RPN expressions. The system dynamically differentiates
    between commands and data using findCommand(), ensuring seamless processing
    of mathematical operations, logical evaluations, and structured code
    execution, reinforcing its stack-driven computational model. There is also
    the main.cpp file that exists to create an instance of the RPNCalc class
    which the client can interact with. Additionally, there is a Makefile used
    for compilation of the .cpp, .h, and .o files into executables in a manner
    that is clean and easy for the user. There are various other .cyl and .cylc
    files that were used for testing (.cylc are compiled .cyl files using the
    cylc executable) that act as parts of rstring inputs to the program and are
    then executed using the file command. Lastly there is the unit_test.h file
    that takes in the RPNCalc class and DatumStack class through it and tests
    various parts of their operations. All .cpp files have a corresponding .h
    file that it includes which acts as a use guide for the class with the
    exception of main.cpp. In order of heirarchy, main.cpp includes RPNCalc.h,
    which takes in DatumStack.h which then takes in Datum.h so that every level
    above any given level can use the information from the class(es) below.  

Data Structures and Algorithms:
    The primary abstract data type (ADT) used in the HuffmanCoder program is a
    binary tree, specifically a Huffman Tree, which is a specialized version
    used for data compression via variable-length prefix coding. This tree is
    implemented using the HuffmanTreeNode class, where each node stores a
    character, a frequency count, and pointers to left and right child nodes. 
    Internally, the Huffman Treecis constructed using a min-heap priority queue
    of HuffmanTreeNode pointers, leveraging a standard priority_queue with a
    custom comparator to order nodes by frequency in ascending order. This
    allows for efficient retrieval of the two lowest-frequency nodes during
    tree construction, each pop() and push() operation taking O(log n) time.
    the choice of a pointer-based binary tree structure allows for dynamic
    construction and recursive traversal but incurs memory overhead due to
    pointer storage and potential data fragmentation. An array-based tree
    representation would reduce pointer overhead and improve cache locality but
    would be unsuitable due to the tree’s unpredictable shape and depth. The
    priority queue offers a well-balanced tradeoff between simplicity and
    performance, ensuring logarithmic operations and intuitive merging during
    tree creation.

    A key algorithm in this implementation is the Huffman Tree construction
    algorithm found in buildTree(), which is responsible for generating the 
    prefix code tree based on character frequencies. First, character 
    frequencies are counted using a hash map (unordered_map<char, int>), a
    structure that offers O(1) average-case insertion and lookup, and each
    entry is converted into a leaf node pushed into the priority queue. Then,
    while more than one node remains, the two nodes with the lowest frequencies
    are removed from the queue, merged into a new internal node with their
    combined frequency, and reinserted into the queue. This greedy algorithm
    continues until one node remains, which becomes the root of the final
    Huffman Tree. The complexity of this algorithm is O(n log n), where n is
    the number of unique characters in the input, due to repeated heap
    operations. The main advantage of this approach is its optimality: Huffman
    coding guarantees the smallest average code length among all prefix-free
    codes for a given frequency distribution. However, it can be memory-
    intensive for large alphabets and does not adapt well to changing data
    distributions without reconstruction. The recursive traversal used in 
    makeCodes() to assign binary codes is simple but powerful, building prefix
    strings in O(n) time and storing them in an unordered_map<char, string> for
    constant-time encoding. On the decoding side, deserialize_tree() and 
    readBits() enable efficient reconstruction of the original text, parsing
    serialized tree strings and decoding the bitstream in linear time. Overall,
    the combination of heap-based tree construction, hash maps for code lookup,
    and recursive traversal for encoding/decoding provides an efficient 
    pipeline for lossless compression. While alternatives like arithmetic 
    coding offer potential gains in specific contexts, the Huffman approach
    balances compression performance, implementation simplicity, and 
    deterministic behavior ideal for file-based encoding tasks.

Testing
    

Part that you found most difficult:
    The way the my implementation of this project works requires that moving 
    left in the tree requries an encoding of 1, but a decoding of 0 and vice 
    versa for movement toward the right side of the tree, it took a lot of 
    testing and a gaining a thorough understanding of every part of the code to
    find that out. 

